@prefix orexis:     <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix mc:     <http://example.org/orexis/microcontroller#> .
@prefix esp32:  <http://example.org/orexis/esp32#> .
@prefix : <http://example.org/orexis/esp32#> .
@prefix wokwi:  <http://example.org/orexis/wokwi#> .
@prefix rdfs:   <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:    <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:    <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<http://example.org/orexis/esp32> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "ESP32" ;
    rdfs:comment """One family of board, two members. A stub: it names the DevKit and the FireBeetle 2 ESP32-E and says how each draws, and does NOT yet describe their headers — the pin positions, which of them can be driven, which sit on ADC1. Those are still literals inside the microcontroller shapes, which is the open seam this package exists to eventually close.

Started here because a picture needs to know which board it is drawing, and that is a fact about the MODEL rather than about the particular board on the windowsill.""" .

:DevKitC a owl:Class ;
    mc:modelName "ESP32-WROOM-32D" ; rdfs:subClassOf mc:Microcontroller ,
        #  The sleep-watcher, stated instead of narrated (#181): the classic ESP32 carries a
        #  ULP-FSM coprocessor — four 16-bit registers, RTC memory, one look per second for
        #  ~150 µA — that can sample ADC1 while the core and the radio deep-sleep, and can do
        #  NOTHING else: it cannot speak the DHT's one-wire timing and this silicon's RTC I2C
        #  is unusable. So one reachable role, and the absence of every other is the honest
        #  half. A fact about the CHIP, so an ESP32-S3 class (RISC-V ULP, working RTC I2C)
        #  would state analog AND the I2C roles — which is what would make a BME280 channel
        #  alarm-watchable on a terrace, and the graph now answers that question by class.
        [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mc:watcherReachesRole ;
          owl:hasValue mc:AnalogInPinRole ] ;
    rdfs:label "ESP32 DevKitC" ;
    wokwi:part "board-esp32-devkit-c-v4" ;
    rdfs:comment "The 38-pin development board, the one with 3V3 on the same header as GPIO 34. Its legs carry their own silkscreen notation, which is what a drawing names them by." .

#  The terrace's board. The SAME silicon as the DevKitC — an ESP32-E module is an ESP32-D0WD-V3
#  with 4 MB flash — so GPIO numbering, the ADC1/ADC2 split, the input-only 34-39 and the flash
#  lines 6-11 are all the plain ESP32's, and the microcontroller shapes' pin rules apply to it
#  unchanged. What differs is the board around the chip, and only what a world can honestly
#  lean on is stated:
#
#    - the sleep-watcher is the same ULP-FSM, so the same one reachable role, for the same
#      reason as above — the BME280's I2C channels on this board promise nothing while it
#      sleeps, and mc:WatchedChannelWiringShape would refuse a world that said otherwise;
#    - the silkscreen is DFRobot's, not Espressif's: GPIO 34 is printed A2, and 21/22 carry
#      SDA/SCL beside their numbers. A world states the notation per pin, as ever.
#
#  What is NOT stated, because nothing here reads it yet: the LiPo charger and the low-power
#  design that make it the outdoor pick, the CH340 serial. A board is described by what a
#  shape or a generator can check. The one built-in part that IS read is below.
:FireBeetle2Esp32E a owl:Class ;
    mc:modelName "DFRobot FireBeetle 2 ESP32-E" ; rdfs:subClassOf mc:Microcontroller ,
        [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mc:watcherReachesRole ;
          owl:hasValue mc:AnalogInPinRole ] ,
        #  The onboard WS2812 — "controlled by pin IO5/D8" (DFRobot wiki, DFR0654). A fact
        #  about the BOARD KIND and not about any unit's wiring: no FireBeetle could lack it and
        #  no world could move it, so it is a hasValue restriction the closure carries to every
        #  unit, exactly as the watcher role above, and NOT a peripheral a hardware.ttl states
        #  with legs and wires — there is nothing to wire. The firmware generator reads it off
        #  the unit and the firmware drives the same outcome vocabulary on it that a world-wired
        #  KY-016 gets; a DevKitC states nothing here and compiles that code away.
        [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty :ws2812Gpio ; owl:hasValue 5 ] ;
    rdfs:label "FireBeetle 2 ESP32-E" ;
    #  No wokwi:part, and the tempting one was tried: Wokwi has no FireBeetle, and drawing it
    #  as "board-esp32-devkit-c-v4" would simulate the firmware faithfully (same chip, same
    #  GPIO numbers). The generator's IMPORT side refused it — a part id claimed by two classes
    #  cannot be resolved back to one, and a draft that guessed would describe a board nobody
    #  has. So a world on this board draws without it, and says so at generation time.
    rdfs:comment "DFRobot's low-power ESP32-E board (DFR0654), the one with a LiPo charger on it — which is why it is the board that goes outside. PlatformIO knows it as dfrobot_firebeetle2_esp32e." .

#  Which line a board kind's built-in addressable RGB LED sits on. A datatype fact stated by
#  restriction on the CLASS, never on a unit: it is what makes a FireBeetle a FireBeetle, and a
#  unit stating a different number would be a different board. Absent on a board with no such
#  LED, which is what "absent" means for every optional part here.
#
#  The board's low-power solder pad, cut for battery use, disconnects this LED from anything but
#  USB (DFRobot wiki: "when the pad is disconnected, you can only drive RGB LED light via the
#  USB Power supply"; ~500 µA static saved). That is a fact about ONE unit's modification and
#  not about the kind, so it is not stated here — the LED is still on GPIO 5 whether or not it
#  lights, and a firmware that writes to it on a cut board wastes nothing worth a term.
:ws2812Gpio a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ; rdfs:label "onboard WS2812 GPIO" ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://wiki.dfrobot.com/FireBeetle_Board_ESP32_E_SKU_DFR0654> .
